    !!! success "The end goal"
        To enable the following services:

        * yourp2ponionaddress.onion:18084 - onion P2P service (for other onion nodes)
        * yourrpconionaddress.onion:18089 - onion RPC service (for wallets connecting over Tor)

        **Onion service for P2P network** is useful for other full node users as it allows them to broadcast transactions over Tor (using `--tx-proxy` option).

        **Onion service for wallet interface** is useful for wallet users connecting over Tor because it mitigates Clearnet and Tor exit node MiTM risks (which are very real). By connecting wallet to an onion service, no MiTM attack is realistic because onion connections are end-to-end encrypted.

    ??? question "Why different P2P ports for clearnet and onion?"
        A: The data served by the Onion p2p port differs from clearnet P2P. A different port is required.

    1. Elevate to root:
        ``` Bash
        sudo su -
        ```
    2. [:link: Install Tor](https://support.torproject.org/apt/#apt_tor-deb-repo)

    3. Add the following lines to `/etc/tor/torrc`. If you already used this guide, replace the old `monerod` hidden service block so it no longer exposes port 18089 on the P2P onion:
        ``` ApacheConf
        HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/monerod
        HiddenServicePort 18084 127.0.0.1:18084    # interface for P2P network

        HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/monerod-rpc
        HiddenServicePort 18089 127.0.0.1:18089    # interface for wallet ("RPC")
        ```
    4. Enable Tor service:
        ``` Bash
        systemctl enable tor
        systemctl restart tor
        ```
    5. View the two onion addresses. The first command prints the P2P address advertised to other nodes; the second prints the RPC address for your wallets:
        ``` Bash
        cat /var/lib/tor/monerod/hostname
        cat /var/lib/tor/monerod-rpc/hostname
        ```
        If you followed the previous version of this guide, your existing onion remains the P2P address. Update your wallets to use the new RPC address after restarting Tor.
    6. Add the P2P address to your Monero [**config file** :link:{ title="Monerod Config" }]{{ config }}, enabling these options:
        ``` ini
        anonymous-inbound=yourp2ponionaddress.onion:18084,127.0.0.1:18084
        tx-proxy=tor,127.0.0.1:9050,disable_noise
        ```
        Replace `yourp2ponionaddress.onion` with the address from `/var/lib/tor/monerod/hostname`. Do not put the RPC address in `anonymous-inbound`.
    7. The node is now available on Tor. You can check that the service is working by using curl:
        ``` Bash
        curl -x socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050 http://yourrpconionaddress.onion:18089/get_info
        ```
    ??? info "Backup Onion keys"
        You may want to back up both keys folders (`/var/lib/tor/monerod` and `/var/lib/tor/monerod-rpc`) to keep control of both onion addresses.
    ??? info "How Tor onion services work?"

        Each `HiddenServiceDir` has its own onion address and key pair. Tor creates the new RPC address in `/var/lib/tor/monerod-rpc` when it restarts. The existing `/var/lib/tor/monerod` address remains the P2P address.
        Tor forwards each onion port to the corresponding local `monerod` port.
